# Conventional Commits (required)

This package publishes with **semantic-release** on merge to `master`. Every
git commit message and PR / squash-merge title **must** follow Conventional
Commits or the wrong version (or no release) will be published.

## Subject format

```
<type>(optional-scope): <short description>
```

- Imperative, concise, no trailing period
- Breaking change: `feat!: …` / `fix!: …` and/or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer

## Type → release (must match `release.config.js`)

| Type | Release |
| ---- | ------- |
| `feat` | **minor** |
| `fix`, `perf`, `revert` | **patch** |
| `feat!` / `fix!` / `BREAKING CHANGE:` | **major** |
| `docs`, `style`, `chore`, `refactor`, `test`, `build`, `ci` | **none** |

## Examples

- `feat: add theme list JSON output`
- `fix(plugin): reject incompatible bermooda.engine ranges`
- `ci: tighten publish workflow permissions`
- `docs: clarify install --source`
- `chore: update vitest`

## Hard rules for agents

- Never commit with free-form subjects (`Update X`, `Fix stuff`, `WIP`,
  `Address comments`).
- Never bump `package.json` `"version"` or run `npm version` / push release
  tags manually — semantic-release owns releases.
- When squash-merging guidance is given, ensure the **PR title** is a valid
  Conventional Commit (that becomes the release-analyzed subject).
- Mixed feature + chore work: use `feat` or `fix` so a release is cut; do not
  hide user-facing changes under `chore` or `ci`.
